Title: "Quotients and Actions"
Id: "442c4a8a-b530-4fe4-8973-1f339026c805"


Text: "Subgroups describe pieces of a group; quotient groups compress pieces
       that are indistinguishable at a chosen scale. Group actions take the
       complementary viewpoint: they represent abstract group elements as
       symmetries of a set. Kernels, normal subgroups, orbits, and stabilizers
       connect these constructions."
Id: "414988da-2898-41e8-8f3f-74753d59eff1"


SectionTitle: "Cosets and Normal Subgroups"
Id: "7c8c60ce-9c18-4ea7-8447-9b47c9118f8b"


[\left.coset:of{H}:by{g}:in{G ::= (X, *, e)}]
Defines: C := \set@{g * h_ : h_ "in" H}
when:
. G is \group
. H \:subgroup:/ G
. g "in" G
Documented:
. called: "left coset of $H?$ by $g?$"
. written: "g?H?"
Id: "b0bd6ac9-937e-46cb-b2f0-9752c5d2a6e0"


[\right.coset:of{H}:by{g}:in{G ::= (X, *, e)}]
Defines: C := \set@{h_ * g : h_ "in" H}
when:
. G is \group
. H \:subgroup:/ G
. g "in" G
Documented:
. called: "right coset of $H?$ by $g?$"
. written: "H?g?"
Id: "152ef2a9-044c-4687-8325-bc80ce2bfa12"


Theorem:
given:
. G is \group
. H \:subgroup:/ G
. g1, g2 "in" G
then:
. oneOf:
  . \left.coset:of{H}:by{g1}:in{G} = \left.coset:of{H}:by{g2}:in{G}
  . \left.coset:of{H}:by{g1}:in{G} \.disjoint.from./ \left.coset:of{H}:by{g2}:in{G}
Documented:
. called: "Left Cosets are Equal or Disjoint"
Id: "4cafbd92-cf4d-4989-ac8f-e53ed45cb238"


[\normal.subgroup:of{G ::= (X, *, e)}]
Declares: N is \group
when: G is \group
satisfies:
. N \:subgroup?:/ G
. forAll: g "in" G
  then:
  . forAll: n "in" G
    where: n "in"? N
    then:
    . exists: m "in" G
      suchThat:
      . m "in"? N
      . g * n = m * g
Documented:
. called: "normal subgroup of $G?$"
. written: "H? \vartriangleleft G?"
. description: "A subgroup is normal when its left and right cosets agree for
                every group element."
Id: "0e5638e1-1109-4980-a652-2e795bed2e44"


TextTheorem: "If $N\trianglelefteq G$, the set $G/N$ of cosets becomes a group
              under $(gN)(hN)=(gh)N$. The identity is $N$ and
              $(gN)^{-1}=g^{-1}N$. If $N$ is not normal, the same formula can
              depend on the representatives $g$ and $h$ and therefore does not
              define a binary operation on the coset set."
Id: "1c5bfe13-52ac-46ae-b75c-af1d8db93224"


[\group.kernel:of{phi(x__)}:from{G1 ::= (X1, *_1, e1)}:to{G2 ::= (X2, *_2, e2)}]
Defines: K := \set@{g_ : g_ "in" G1 | phi(g_) = e2}
when:
. G1, G2 is \group
. phi is \group.homomorphism:from{G1}:to{G2}
Documented:
. called: "kernel of $phi?$"
. written: "\ker(phi?)"
Id: "dbdd8045-535e-4d8b-b4d1-2f10e2d2648f"


[\group.image:of{phi(x__)}:from{G1 ::= (X1, *_1, e1)}:to{G2 ::= (X2, *_2, e2)}]
Defines: I := \set@{phi(g_) : g_ "in" G1}
when:
. G1, G2 is \group
. phi is \group.homomorphism:from{G1}:to{G2}
Documented:
. called: "image of $phi?$"
. written: "\operatorname{im}(phi?)"
Id: "f13b0e2b-be95-4735-b963-fbc91db6b175"


TextTheorem: "First isomorphism theorem for groups. For every homomorphism
              $\varphi:G\to H$, the map $g\ker\varphi\mapsto\varphi(g)$ is a
              well-defined group isomorphism
              $G/\ker\varphi\cong\operatorname{im}\varphi$. The theorem says
              that every homomorphic image is obtained by identifying exactly
              those elements whose quotient lies in the kernel."
Id: "033fb304-f310-43fa-84fb-2070d17de0e2"


SectionTitle: "Group Actions"
Id: "3033f57e-9ff5-4da7-bf72-b3c09c27f533"


[\group.action:of{G ::= (Y, *, e)}:on{X}]
Declares: alpha(g_, x_) is \function:on{Y \.set.cross./ X}:to{X}
when:
. G is \group
. X is \set
satisfies:
. forAll: x "in" X
  then: alpha(e, x) = x
. forAll: g, h "in" G
  then:
  . forAll: x "in" X
    then: alpha(g * h, x) = alpha(g, alpha(h, x))
Documented:
. called: "group action of $G?$ on $X?$"
. writing: alpha(g_, x_)
  as: "g_? \cdot x_?"
. description: "A left action represents each group element as a symmetry of the
                set and respects identity and multiplication."
Id: "fa043cd6-0ce8-4504-82bb-aba9ad2c8a30"


[\orbit:of{x}:under{alpha}:of{G}:on{X}]
Defines: O := \set@{alpha(g_, x) : g_ "in" G}
when:
. G is \group
. X is \set
. alpha is \group.action:of{G}:on{X}
. x "in" X
Documented:
. called: "orbit of $x?$ under $alpha?$"
. written: "G? \cdot x?"
Id: "373a9fce-21a7-4155-9f1d-cfb662941906"


[\stabilizer:of{x}:under{alpha}:of{G}:on{X}]
Defines: S := \set@{g_ : g_ "in" G | alpha(g_, x) = x}
when:
. G is \group
. X is \set
. alpha is \group.action:of{G}:on{X}
. x "in" X
Documented:
. called: "stabilizer of $x?$ under $alpha?$"
. written: "G?_{x?}"
Id: "02a3fe2e-dbbf-49a6-8cf2-cc5cc85568f7"


TextTheorem: "Orbit--stabilizer theorem. If a finite group $G$ acts on a set
              $X$, then for every $x\in X$ one has $|G|=|G\cdot x|\,|G_x|$. More
              generally, the map $G/G_x\to G\cdot x$ given by
              $gG_x\mapsto g\cdot x$ is a well-defined bijection."
Id: "8b9d1618-6fe0-4cd1-a338-3eb2e3242b9a"


Text: "An action turns structural questions into counting and geometry. A
       transitive action has a single orbit and presents $X$ as a coset space.
       Conjugation of $G$ on itself has conjugacy classes as orbits and
       centralizers as stabilizers. These examples explain why quotient groups
       and actions are not separate topics but two views of the same symmetry
       mechanism."
Id: "f873aaf6-708d-4dc9-a895-0a82e2ca658c"
